hi there this is steve uh and this is  the second in my  series steve's summer sessions where i  hope to be able to answer questions uh  put to me by those of you who follow my  youtube channel  and of course as you'll remember i ask  you to submit these questions via  twitter  ask steve  so last week i spoke about reading  comprehension  and getting to output  and today there are two questions that  i'm going to try to handle one is  do i recommend studying two languages at  the same time  and the second one is what do i think of  space repetition systems or srs systems  and including sentence mining  two languages i'm often asked this  question  i think the most important factor there  is  what you yourself like to do because i  know there are people who are quite  successful at studying two three four  languages at the same time i've seen the  video of professor argez  where he has  i don't know how many languages on the  go at the same time and they are all  accomplished language learners  personally  i find it very difficult  because i find that starting a language  from scratch is a major commitment it's  an emotional commitment a commitment in  time in focus in energy  and i find it very difficult to direct  that kind of focus energy emotion to two  or three languages at the same time i  prefer to focus on one for a period of  time six months twelve months then focus  on another  uh if i have a language let's take my  present situation i have spent the last  six seven weeks  focused on romanian  prior to that i was focused on korean  so having dropped my korean i will slide  a bit but when i come back from romania  and i focus on korean again i won't have  lost very much but if i had tried to to  do two of them i don't think i would  have made any significant progress  in romanian  uh i believe it's possible to  have a very heavy heavy emphasis on one  language the one that you're starting  with and to spend 15-20 percent of your  time in a sort of maintenance activity  trying to not slip too far behind in one  or two other languages and i have been  doing this i occasionally read something  in czech occasionally read something in  in russian out of interest  but those are languages that i'm further  ahead in i don't think that i could have  80 of my time on romanian and 20 of my  time on korean because in both those  languages i'm not sufficiently  along in the language that i could treat  it as a minor activity both of those  languages require a lot of focus so in  my own case if it's a matter of learning  a language from scratch i would like to  go at least eighty percent if not a  hundred percent full time full bore full  focus on learning that language once i  achieve a certain level in the language  then it's possible to diversify so if i  wanted to if i leave a romanian and  korean out of it where i'm still at the  sort of beginner advanced beginner stage  um russian and czech i could do russian  czech and portuguese at the same time  because these are maintenance activities  because at that time  my learning activity is more a matter of  enjoying the language i'm already at the  stage now where i simply want to enjoy  an interesting podcast in czech or  portuguese or russian  because of the subject matter because it  brings me up to date on events in brazil  or portugal or russia or the czech  republic so it's just an enjoyment thing  and if i run into people where i can use  the language i speak the language so  those are not the sort of  hard core heavy lifting that you have to  do when you start in the language if you  are at that hardcore heavy lifting  rather  you know  you know forgetting a lot continuing to  make the same mistakes  not feeling confident in the language  there i motivated to get better i'm  motivated motivated to move up to that  next  shelf that next level and therefore i  tend to commit myself full time so there  you have it that's my take on it if  you're at the early stages in the  language i would recommend based on my  experience  that you do one at a time all right  second question was space represent  space repetition systems known as srs  there's um  i can't remember there's an key  there's uh gosh  memorize there are a number of them out  there we have  in link we have a spaced repetition  system as part of the package of  functions that  we provide  there are many people who swear by anki  it's probably the most popular to judge  by what i hear  uh you even have the gold list which was  put forward by  david jones i believe his name was  where you write certain things out and  there's a certain repetition  um  period and it's all based on the idea  that  that uh if you review certain some  things within a certain period of time  you won't have a chance to completely  forget it that there is and there are  even algorithms which apparently are  scientifically derived  and and therefore ensure that you will  revisit a word or a phrase uh within  that sort of  period of time that's specified by the  algorithm  okay personally i spend very little time  with those kinds of systems and that's  not to say that they don't work for some  people  or maybe for many people i don't know  but if you are asking me what i think of  them i find them uninteresting  you know  i don't have my statistics handy but i  would suspect that in romanian in the  last seven months  i have  looked up and therefore linked on our  system  gosh i don't know 20 000 items  uh like lots like i'll go in and read an  item and i'll go through and if there's  200 new words 150 of them bing bing bing  bing bing i'm linking them in our system  and then i go away and read it on ipad  and if i go i can go through five of  those in a day so now i'm at 500 600 in  one day  i don't think an srs system uh would  keep up with me so pretty soon i'd have  an imagine unmanageable  uh list  deck i think as they call them  uh to try to go through  so  that  that's one problem the other problem